\DOC PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC : int -> int list -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal over one block of machine instructions,
replacing the assumptions that describe the current state by assumptions
describing a later state and retargeting the {eventually} conclusion at that
later state.

\DESCRIBE
{PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC n shf s} takes {n : int}, an index used in forming the
state names/offsets for the step; {shf : int list}, a list of integer
parameters (a singleton in all observed traces) controlling the step; and
{s : int}, an integer expression (typically built from {n}) giving the step's
position, which fixes which instructions are executed and hence the numbering
of the resulting state. Assumptions not about the state (arithmetic side
conditions, invariants relating the abstract values) are kept unchanged; every
assumption mentioning the current state variable — the program-image
assumption, the register/flag/memory {read} equations, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} relation — is discharged and replaced by the corresponding
assumptions about a fresh, later state variable, with their right-hand sides
rewritten into the form produced by executing the block. The {eventually}
conclusion is re-applied to the new state variable, its predicate and
{MAYCHANGE} part unchanged. Writing {R} and {R'} for the state-describing
assumption sets:
{
   A, R(s_i) ?- eventually ... s_i
   ================================  PACKED_DIVSTEP_TAC n shf s
   A, R'(s_j) ?- eventually ... s_j
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this shape: a machine-state symbolic-execution goal whose conclusion
is an {eventually} claim about the current state.

\ENDDOC
